## Introduction

The project aims to describe every detected protein domain and identify candidates with potentially novel structures. The workflow begins with existing protein structures, predicts consensus domains, annotates them with 53 PETadex HMMs and classifies them using structural references. Unassigned representatives pass through quality and novelty screens.

A PETadex hit is sequence-family evidence; structural novelty is a separate assessment. Neither alone establishes PET-degrading activity. Domains excluded from novelty screening remain in the complete catalogue. HMM acceptance is currently unset, so reported hits do not change TED filtering.

## Objectives & Methods

1. Recover high/medium-consensus domains with Merizo, UniDoc and Chainsaw, using the released numbering correction.
2. Annotate every domain against the 53-model PETadex library.
3. Cluster sequences before CATH classification and cluster removal.
4. Screen unassigned representatives using structural quality, structural clustering, PDB/domain-library comparisons, symmetry, chopping, joint clustering, sensitive search and the released final recipe.
5. Preserve domain lineage, annotations, exclusions, survivors and wall-clock timings.

GPU segmentation uses the four configured GPUs; embedding/chopping use CUDA. CPU allocation follows available affinity/quota. Native tools and database environments remain the validated VM installations. This notebook's local analysis environment does not recreate those specialized installations.

The base configuration is loaded from the existing validated run. Database paths, releases and reconstruction conventions are inherited and recorded in each new run. `exact_TED_reproduction` remains false; confidence interpretation is provisional.

