## Results & Discussion

### Earlier observed results (not outputs of this copy)

| Run | Proteins | Consensus domains | PETadex reported-hit domains | Final representatives |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| `ted_random100_j3w39z72_petadex_v1` | 100 | 162 | 115 | 0 |
| `ted_positive5_current_validation_v1` | 5 | 14 | 12 | 2 |

The random-100 run had 13 unassigned representatives, four passed quality and all four were excluded at PDB screening. The five-control run recovered all five published domain boundaries exactly; two survived and three were excluded at chopping, potentially through representative lineage.

These five positives are a small selected control set, not a general sensitivity estimate. An earlier run had a different joint-clustering outcome; the source of that discrepancy remains unresolved. Do not tune thresholds solely to recover these controls.

### Follow-up questions

- Which matches and coverage values explain each exclusion?
- How often do representative decisions remove members that would pass individually?
- What calibrated significance/coverage rules should be used for PETadex annotation?
- Are reference releases, coordinate preparation and cluster ordering sufficient to explain differences across runs?

