## 5. Execution order and numerical rules

These are the implemented stages; the driver runs them in this order. A tool failure stops the run. A genuinely empty candidate set skips downstream tool invocations and is reported as empty, not as a successfully executed search.

| Order | Stage and rule |
|---|---|
| 01–02 | Validate all-atom, single-chain PDBs and input hashes; run Merizo → inherited-chopping UniDoc → Chainsaw; consensus IoU 0.7 and released offset=1; retain high/medium; parser/consensus minimum segment 5 and domain 25 residues. |
| 03 | MMseqs2 easy-linclust: identity 0.5, coverage 0.9, coverage mode 5. Save every membership. |
| 04 | Foldseek CATH search on **all members**: sensitivity 10, E-value 0.108662, coverage 0.366757, mode 5. H: TM>0.56, coverage>0.367, E<0.019. T: TM>0.42, coverage>0.786. Score/coverage field choices remain configurable above. |
| 05 | Unmatched domains: FoldClass embedding → cosine-nearest CATH reference → direct TM-align; query-normalized TM>0.5 assigns a fold. Then remove any sequence cluster containing an assigned member. |
| 06 | Quality: normalized Rg<0.356, packing per chosen policy, ≥6 helices/strands, pLDDT80≥90. |
| 07 | First Foldseek clustering: -s 4, -e 0.001, -c 0.7, cov-mode 5, TM 0.56, identity 0.2, seq-id-mode 1, cluster-mode 1. |
| 08 | Second structural clustering: coverage 0.8, TM threshold 0.8, cluster-mode 1. |
| 09 | PDB TM-alignment search: retain unmatched after query TM>0.56 and query coverage>0.6. |
| 10 | Exhaustive domain-library search: match if query TM>0.56 and query/target coverage>0.6. |
| 11 | SymD: scores>9 enter the separate high-symmetry branch. |
| 12 | Chopping score≥0.5 AND DOM single-domain output (1 on 1 domains, 100% agreement). |
| 13 | Joint clustering with domain libraries: TM threshold 0.5, coverage 0.6, alignment-type 1. Keep clusters without reference members. |
| 14 | Sensitive exhaustive Foldseek: (max(query TM, target TM)>0.5 OR RMSD<3) AND query coverage>0.6. Keep unmatched. |
| 16 | Released exhaustive Foldclass recipe with native TM-align `-fast`: all eligible official embedding entries, cosine floor −1; aligned length ≥0.6×query length and max(TM)≥0.5 and RMSD<1000 rejects. Original 2000-residue embedding/sequence truncation and first-hit stopping are retained. |
| Final | Rank survivors by mean embedding distance to 50 nearest references; export representative structures/paths, sequences, lineage and decisions. |

GPU use: four concurrent segmentation workers; Merizo explicitly uses CUDA and Chainsaw selects CUDA in the isolated environment. UniDoc/STRIDE run on CPU. FoldClass and the chopping model use the configured CUDA device. The pinned Foldseek, MMseqs2 clustering, SymD, DOM and TM-align stages use CPUs; idle GPUs during those stages are expected.

CPU allocation: Foldseek/MMseqs2 search, clustering, database creation/export and joint clustering receive the detected `threads` value on new runs. GPU segmentation workers divide that CPU budget. Final native comparisons use one worker per available logical CPU. Database concatenation stays single-threaded to preserve correspondence between sequence, structure, coordinates and headers. Single-threaded tools and small/GPU-bound stages cannot be made to saturate every CPU by a setting alone.


**Added after segmentation:** `02_petadex_hmm` scans every consensus-domain sequence against 53 HMMs, before clustering removes or groups domains. Raw reporting uses sequence E ≤10 and conditional domain E ≤10, matching the exploratory scan; these are deliberately permissive reporting limits. No GA/TC/NC threshold is assumed. Domain independent E-values and per-hit span coverage are retained. Accepted-family, partial-hit and unresolved statuses are separate, and the existing TED survival decision is retained.


## 9. Domain catalogue and HMM evidence

For annotation-only mode, take the output path printed in the log. For RUN mode, outputs are in the new batch run.

- `domain_catalogue.tsv`: every consensus domain, parent, sequence, parent positions, structural classification evidence, TED outcome, PETadex assessment, model names, best hit, and combined novelty status.
- `petadex_matches.tsv`: all reported alignment rows, E-values, score, model/query coordinates and span coverage; parent-position mapping handles discontinuous domains.
- `petadex_annotations.json`: full parsed evidence, actual command, model count, checksum, and acceptance policy.
- `ted_survivors_with_petadex.tsv`: original TED survivors with sequence-family evidence. Not a list of confirmed novel domains.
- `petadex_summary.json`: annotation totals and policy.

When acceptance is unset, a TED survivor is marked `HMM_acceptance_unresolved`, including when no hit was reported. With an explicit policy, accepted family matches and partial matches are distinguished from candidates. These settings do not silently remove entire sequence/structural clusters. Existing TED outcome files and positive-control validation retain their original meaning.


## Scope and validation

Both scientific patches remain active. CPU scheduling changes execution rather than thresholds. The original released script still computes the embeddings, cosine ordering, length mask and first-hit decision. A bounded pool prepares upcoming native comparisons. Results are consumed in original order; successful speculative work may be cached but does not select the reported hit. Old audit records lack sequence identity; reused records report that field as unknown, never a fabricated value. Sequence identity is not used in this final acceptance rule.

No-hit results require every eligible reference to be consumed successfully. A timeout, malformed score, changed reference or missing result fails the stage. Pair caches are tied to the query, native binary, catalog identity and reference manifest. The resume patch is recorded separately from the original engine identity; future runs use the CPU-aware engine fingerprint.

All-CPU execution can be limited by disk throughput, available independent work and single-threaded code. Database concatenation remains at one thread for correctness. CPU affinity/cgroup limits are honored. Changing resource allocations after a run starts can change its identity; use resume mode for the existing run rather than editing its configuration.

The reference releases remain substitutes, and only the C5NV13 control has been reported as validated end-to-end so far. Five-control results below are measured, not assumed. Exact historical reproduction and full biological validation remain false.


PETadex is a project-specific annotation extension, not an original TED step. No calibrated PETadex cutoffs were supplied. Low HMM coverage can reflect a larger model rather than a spurious match; retain partial evidence for review. Searching a concatenated discontinuous domain can differ from searching a full chain. Full-chain scanning is not part of this extension.

## Environment setup — run once, then switch kernel

Run the cell below to create a repository-local `.venv` from `requirements.txt` and register the **igem-toronto** kernel. Switch to that kernel before using the notebook. The dependencies here support orchestration, tables and figures. The scientific pipeline still calls the validated TED Python, segmentation environment and native executables on the VM.

This cell is disabled by default to avoid reinstalling dependencies during Run All. Set `SETUP_ENVIRONMENT = True` once. Registering this kernel name updates its user-level registration. After setup, switch kernels and return this flag to False.

from pathlib import Path
import subprocess, sys, datetime
SETUP_ENVIRONMENT = False
_here = Path.cwd().resolve()
repo = _here.parent if _here.name == "notebooks" else _here
requirements = repo / "requirements.txt"
venv = repo / ".venv"
if SETUP_ENVIRONMENT:
    assert requirements.is_file(), f"Missing {requirements}; extract the full package into the repository."
    if not venv.exists():
        subprocess.run([sys.executable, "-m", "venv", str(venv)], check=True)
    python = venv / "bin" / "python"
    subprocess.run([str(python), "-m", "pip", "install", "-r", str(requirements)], check=True)
    subprocess.run([str(python), "-m", "ipykernel", "install", "--user", "--name", "igem-toronto",
                    "--display-name", "igem-toronto"], check=True)
    work = repo / "files" / "261010_issue59_ted_petadex"
    work.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    frozen = subprocess.check_output([str(python), "-m", "pip", "freeze"], text=True)
    (work / "analysis_environment_freeze.txt").write_text(frozen)
    print("Setup complete. Switch to igem-toronto, set this flag False, then run the launch cell.")
else:
    print("Setup skipped. Enable once if the igem-toronto kernel is not installed.")
